    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "All green. Here's a summary of the change.\n\n## What changed\n\nThe owner `customerCode` is now `'<REGION>-<HASH8>'` (e.g. `NSW-16E6609E`), and every value derived from it follows the new region-and-hash identity.\n\n**New / reused logic**\n- **`util/Sha256Hex.java`** (new) \u2014 a single shared helper producing upper-case hex SHA-256 prefixes. The duplicate hashing code that lived privately in `HouseholdIdGenerator` was removed and now delegates here, so both identifier generators share one implementation.\n- **`rest/CustomerCodeGenerator.java`** \u2014 `generate(region, telephone, lastName)` now returns `region + \"-\" + Sha256Hex.upperHexPrefix(telephone + lastName, 8)`; the per-city sequence and city/last-name prefixes are gone. It also gained a static `regionOf(customerCode)` that parses the region segment back out (single owner of the code's format).\n\n**Consumers updated**\n- **`OwnerRestControllerV1`** \u2014 computes `region = LocalityResolver.localityOf(postcode, city)` and passes the normalized telephone and last name to the generator. (The `cityOwnerCount` lookup stays \u2014 it still enforces the per-city limit.)\n- **`OwnerMapper.formatLocality`** \u2014 locality now derives from the region embedded in the customer code via `CustomerCodeGenerator.regionOf(...)`, instead of resolving postcode/city again.\n- **Membership number, check digit, and the create audit record** needed no code changes \u2014 they already build off `customerCode`, so they automatically move to the new identity (Luhn runs over the hex code's digits, membership number becomes `NSW-3F2A9C1E-M26`, and the audit line logs the new code).\n\n**Docs** \u2014 `openapi.yml` descriptions/examples and the relevant javadoc were updated to the new format.\n\nEverything compiles and the full test suite passes (`BUILD SUCCESS`), including the `coreCustomerCodeIsRegionHash` acceptance test.",
